WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Next, let's talk about the four basic steps to first aid care.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.240
Step one is scene safety.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.560
Your very first priority is your own safety and the patient's.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.240
Scan the area for environmental hazards like fire, traffic, or water.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.400
If the scene is unsafe, do not approach, just call for professional help and wait for them

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.559
to arrive.

00:00:22.559 --> 00:00:25.440
Remember, you can't help if you're hurt.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.760
This is also a good time to put on any personal protective equipment if available.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.840
If you must move the person, either to perform CPR or get them to safety, do it quickly.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.880
If you suspect a neck or spinal injury and have a second person to help, try to protect

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.480
their head and neck while you move them.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.919
Step two is to identify the emergency.

00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:48.380
Remember, people won't always be able to tell you that something is wrong.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.520
Check their responsiveness and breathing and observe them for any sudden changes in their

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.480
appearance or behavior.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.560
If the person is responsive, introduce yourself and ask if you can help.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.760
If they are unresponsive, we assume consent, meaning we act as if they want our help.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.160
Step three is to call 9-1-1 and get an AED.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.040
If there are others around, tell someone specifically to call 9-1-1 and have them find an AED.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.000
If you are alone, call 9-1-1 and place them on speakerphone.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.080
Step four is to provide care.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.400
You are essentially the caretaker until professional help takes over the scene.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.839
In the next few slides, we're going to walk through exactly what that care looks like

00:01:27.839 --> 00:01:29.760
for different injuries and emergencies.

